The M6 command-gate half — Attack/AttackMove/Stop stop being stubs and
start meaning what combat needs:

- Move: unchanged (Move capability only).
- AttackMove: split from Move — now requires BOTH Move and Attack per
  unit. A unit with Attack but no Move (e.g. the Turret) is refused:
  AttackMove is a movement command, the turret cannot move. The
  Turret can still receive Attack on a specific target. Apply pushes
  a MoveTo (the engage-en-route semantics are the combat pipeline's
  auto-acquisition, which fires on any unit with Attack that has no
  commanded target — the unit moves toward the MoveTo target and
  engages enemies within acquire_range along the way).
- Attack: per-unit Attack capability check (replaces the
  `check_capability_present` stub). The target must exist and be
  visible to the issuer (NotVisible was already wired in
  `check_target`); plus an explicit ownership check so the issuer
  cannot Attack its own entity (the visibility check would otherwise
  pass for own entities, since friendlies are always visible). Apply
  pushes `Order::AttackUnit { target }` and sets the Attack
  capability's `target` slot immediately so the combat pipeline
  (stage 7, this same tick) can hit if the target is in range.
- Stop: unchanged validation (permissive — legal for any owned
  entity). Apply now also clears any Attack target slot — Stop means
  "stand down", not just "stop moving".

The unused `check_capability_present` stub is removed (the
exhaustive match keeps the compiler honest for future variants).

Encoding unchanged (v4) — no new state, only new command-gate paths.
